"[O]ne is not to have his liberty of expression in appropriate places
abridged on the plea that it may be exercised in some other place."
>From _Schneider v. Town of Irvington_, 308 U.S. 147, 163 (1939).

_The Right to Protest_  (an ACLU Handbook) by Joel M. Gora, et al. says:

"[Q] Can officials in charge of a traditional public forums permanently
designate a portion of the forum for demonstrations and declare the
rest of the forum to be out-of-bounds?"

"[A} If officials wished to make a permanent designation of a portion
of a traditional public forums for demonstrations, they would have to
establish two things. First, they would have to prove that the
designated portions of the forum permits demonstrators to reach their
intended audience approximately as effectively as the out-of-bounds
portion would. Second, they would have to establish identifiable
characteristics of the out-of-bounds portion of the forum that make it
unsuitable for demonstrations and that make the designed area more
suitable. The designation of portions of traditional public forums for
speech activities was rejected by the courts in _Collin v. Chicago
Park District_. There park officials designed four forums areas out of
a citywide park system for which no permit would be required. The rest
of the system was limited to use for picnicking and other recreational
activities. Chicago Park District officials contended they could
prohibit controversial demonstrations in nondesigned portions of the
park system because they were inconsistent with the recreational uses
of these same portions. This arrangement was held to be a violation of
the First Amendment because it has the effect of denying controversial
speakers meaningful access to their intended audience."

